1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,919
One point zero one is only
a tiny bit more than one.

2
00:00:04,319 --> 00:00:07,063
Multiply it by itself a thousand times.

3
00:00:07,063 --> 00:00:09,000
Will it beat ten thousand?

4
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:10,671
Aarav guessed no.

5
00:00:10,671 --> 00:00:14,354
A tiny step, even a thousand
times, cannot go that far.

6
00:00:14,354 --> 00:00:15,254
Can it?

7
00:00:15,391 --> 00:00:18,223
His calculator would
need a thousand presses.

8
00:00:18,223 --> 00:00:20,474
Is there a smarter way to decide?

9
00:00:20,974 --> 00:00:21,874
There is.

10
00:00:21,775 --> 00:00:25,982
Today the binomial theorem compares
huge numbers, and finds the middle

11
00:00:25,982 --> 00:00:26,882
term.

12
00:00:27,178 --> 00:00:31,134
Write one point zero one as
one plus zero point zero one.

13
00:00:31,134 --> 00:00:33,909
Now it is a binomial, with two parts.

14
00:00:34,309 --> 00:00:39,337
So one point zero one, to the power
one thousand, is one plus zero point

15
00:00:39,337 --> 00:00:42,058
zero one, to the power one thousand.

16
00:00:42,458 --> 00:00:45,035
Every term of this expansion is positive.

17
00:00:45,035 --> 00:00:47,915
So adding terms can only
make the total grow.

18
00:00:48,565 --> 00:00:51,333
Watch the terms add up, one by one.

19
00:00:51,733 --> 00:00:53,427
The first term is one.

20
00:00:53,427 --> 00:00:58,028
The second is one thousand times
zero point zero one, which is ten.

21
00:00:58,428 --> 00:01:00,298
The third is about fifty.

22
00:01:00,298 --> 00:01:02,646
Then about one hundred sixty six.

23
00:01:02,646 --> 00:01:04,350
The terms keep growing.

24
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:07,890
By the eleventh term, the
total passes ten thousand.

25
00:01:07,890 --> 00:01:10,715
And there are still more
positive terms to add.

26
00:01:11,365 --> 00:01:16,640
So one point zero one, to the power one
thousand, is bigger than ten thousand.

27
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:19,534
In fact, it is about twenty one thousand.

28
00:01:20,184 --> 00:01:22,791
Here is the example from the NCERT book.

29
00:01:22,791 --> 00:01:27,947
Which is larger: one point zero one, to
the power one million, or ten thousand?

30
00:01:28,347 --> 00:01:29,247
Step one.

31
00:01:29,112 --> 00:01:33,348
Write it as one plus zero point
zero one, to the power one million.

32
00:01:34,098 --> 00:01:34,998
Step two.

33
00:01:34,899 --> 00:01:38,574
The second term is one million
times zero point zero one.

34
00:01:38,574 --> 00:01:40,529
That is exactly ten thousand.

35
00:01:41,279 --> 00:01:42,179
Step three.

36
00:01:42,175 --> 00:01:45,322
One plus ten thousand,
plus more positive terms.

37
00:01:45,322 --> 00:01:47,631
So it is bigger than ten thousand.

38
00:01:47,631 --> 00:01:49,180
Two terms were enough.

39
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:50,867
Now you try.

40
00:01:50,867 --> 00:01:55,631
Is one point one, to the power ten
thousand, bigger than one thousand?

41
00:01:55,631 --> 00:01:56,869
Pause and think.

42
00:02:00,069 --> 00:02:00,969
Yes!

43
00:02:00,568 --> 00:02:05,444
The second term is ten thousand times
zero point one, which is one thousand.

44
00:02:05,444 --> 00:02:07,858
Add one, and it is already bigger.

45
00:02:08,508 --> 00:02:09,839
Now, the middle term.

46
00:02:09,839 --> 00:02:11,941
Think of a row of seats in a cinema.

47
00:02:12,341 --> 00:02:15,034
Seven seats have one
middle seat, the fourth.

48
00:02:15,034 --> 00:02:18,542
Eight seats have two middle
seats, the fourth and the fifth.

49
00:02:18,942 --> 00:02:20,882
An expansion is the same.

50
00:02:20,882 --> 00:02:24,676
a plus b, to the power
n, has n plus one terms.

51
00:02:25,326 --> 00:02:26,767
So the rule depends on n.

52
00:02:27,117 --> 00:02:29,659
If n is even, n plus one is odd.

53
00:02:29,659 --> 00:02:31,497
There is one middle term.

54
00:02:31,497 --> 00:02:34,397
It is term number n over two, plus one.

55
00:02:35,147 --> 00:02:37,644
If n is odd, n plus one is even.

56
00:02:37,644 --> 00:02:39,549
There are two middle terms.

57
00:02:39,549 --> 00:02:43,541
Term n plus one over two, and
term n plus three over two.

58
00:02:44,291 --> 00:02:47,402
And the middle term has the
biggest number in front.

59
00:02:47,402 --> 00:02:50,080
It sits at the top of the hill of n C r.

60
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:51,785
Side by side.

61
00:02:51,785 --> 00:02:54,161
Take a plus b, to the power six.

62
00:02:54,161 --> 00:02:58,394
That is seven terms, so the fourth
term is the only middle term.

63
00:02:58,794 --> 00:03:01,051
Now a plus b, to the power seven.

64
00:03:01,051 --> 00:03:05,364
That is eight terms, so the fourth
and the fifth are both middle terms.

65
00:03:05,965 --> 00:03:06,865
Quick one.

66
00:03:06,765 --> 00:03:10,705
How many middle terms does a
plus b, to the power nine, have?

67
00:03:10,705 --> 00:03:11,883
Pause and decide.

68
00:03:15,083 --> 00:03:15,983
Two!

69
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:17,954
Nine is odd, so there are ten terms.

70
00:03:17,954 --> 00:03:20,734
The fifth and the sixth
are the middle terms.

71
00:03:21,384 --> 00:03:22,464
Remember this.

72
00:03:22,464 --> 00:03:23,818
n plus one terms.

73
00:03:23,818 --> 00:03:25,687
Even n, one middle term.

74
00:03:25,687 --> 00:03:27,556
Odd n, two middle terms.

75
00:03:28,256 --> 00:03:29,946
Now, one worked example.

76
00:03:30,296 --> 00:03:33,834
Find the middle term of x
plus two, to the power eight.

77
00:03:34,334 --> 00:03:35,234
Step one.

78
00:03:35,093 --> 00:03:36,955
n is eight, which is even.

79
00:03:36,955 --> 00:03:39,909
So there is one middle
term, term number five.

80
00:03:40,659 --> 00:03:41,559
Step two.

81
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:43,325
Term five means r is four.

82
00:03:43,325 --> 00:03:47,925
So it is eight C four, times x to
the four, times two to the four.

83
00:03:48,675 --> 00:03:49,575
Step three.

84
00:03:49,551 --> 00:03:51,219
Eight C four is seventy.

85
00:03:51,219 --> 00:03:53,086
Two to the four is sixteen.

86
00:03:53,086 --> 00:03:57,364
So the middle term is one thousand
one hundred twenty x to the four.

87
00:03:58,014 --> 00:03:59,475
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:03:59,825 --> 00:04:03,294
Find the middle term of x
plus one, to the power seven.

89
00:04:03,294 --> 00:04:05,719
Many students give only the fourth term.

90
00:04:06,119 --> 00:04:10,384
But seven is odd, so there are
eight terms and two middle terms.

91
00:04:10,384 --> 00:04:14,965
The fourth and the fifth: thirty
five x to the four, and thirty five x

92
00:04:14,965 --> 00:04:15,865
cubed.

93
00:04:16,216 --> 00:04:17,502
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:17,502 --> 00:04:19,765
Comparing numbers is in the NCERT book.

95
00:04:19,765 --> 00:04:23,824
The middle term is not in the current
NCERT text, but JEE Main asks it,

96
00:04:23,824 --> 00:04:26,968
for example comparing the
middle terms of two expansions.

97
00:04:27,568 --> 00:04:28,733
Exam level now.

98
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:31,319
Find the middle term of this expansion.

99
00:04:31,319 --> 00:04:32,533
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:37,933 --> 00:04:39,248
The answer is A.

101
00:04:39,248 --> 00:04:41,440
One thousand one hundred twenty.

102
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:45,210
n is eight, so there is
one middle term, the fifth.

103
00:04:45,210 --> 00:04:46,229
So r is four.

104
00:04:46,729 --> 00:04:51,919
The term is eight C four, times two
x, to the four, times minus one over

105
00:04:51,919 --> 00:04:53,336
x, to the four.

106
00:04:53,336 --> 00:04:54,530
The x's cancel.

107
00:04:55,030 --> 00:05:00,248
Seventy, times sixteen, times plus one,
is one thousand one hundred twenty.

108
00:05:00,248 --> 00:05:03,096
Minus one to an even power is plus one.

109
00:05:03,696 --> 00:05:05,236
Here is your recap.

110
00:05:05,236 --> 00:05:06,819
Screenshot this card.

111
00:05:10,019 --> 00:05:12,205
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

112
00:05:12,605 --> 00:05:13,505
One.

113
00:05:13,101 --> 00:05:17,782
Why does every extra term make one
plus x, to the n, bigger, when x is

114
00:05:17,782 --> 00:05:18,682
positive?

115
00:05:19,422 --> 00:05:20,322
Two.

116
00:05:19,857 --> 00:05:23,595
How many middle terms does a plus
b, to the power twelve, have?

117
00:05:24,495 --> 00:05:25,395
Three.

118
00:05:25,043 --> 00:05:29,074
Which terms are the middle terms
of a plus b, to the power eleven?

119
00:05:30,174 --> 00:05:32,762
Next time: a number pattern with a rule.

120
00:05:32,762 --> 00:05:34,691
Can you guess what comes next?

121
00:05:34,691 --> 00:05:36,566
We start sequences and series.
